<title>SENEGAL: West African central bank lowers leading rates.</title>
<headline>West African central bank lowers leading rates.</headline>
<dateline>DAKAR 1997-02-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>The West African Central Bank (BCEAO) has lowered its leading rates by a quarter of a point, thanks to continuing good macro-economic indicators within the West African Monetary Union (UMOA), it said in a statement.</p>
<p>The discount rate went down from 6.50 to 6.25 percent and the two to seven day lending rate was lowered from 6.00 to 5.75 percent. The changes took effect from February 17.</p>
<p>&quot;This decision shows that the Central Bank is continuing to support its investment and production stimulation programme in UMOA member countries, with the aim of consolidating economic growth via a cut in the cost of financing,&quot; the statement said.</p>
<p>The central bank and governments within the region are keen to lower interest rates in order to encourage economic growth.</p>
<p>The West African Monetary Union member countries are: Benin, Burkina Faso, Ivory Coast, Mali, Niger, Senegal and Togo.</p>
<p>-- Clare Black, Abidjan newsroom +225 21 90 90</p>
